<RevealLayout
  title="The 2x2x2 Rubik's Cube"
  description="Efficient representations and traversal of the Rubik's Cube state space"
  theme="dark"
>
  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        The Rubik's Cube has an absurd number of possible configurations. Far
        too many to explore, even with a modern computer. But what about its
        little brother, the 2 by 2? Is its universe small enough to explore
        completely?
      </p>
      <p>
        And not just explore it. Can we compute the minimum number of moves
        needed to solve every single cube? Spoiler: yes, we can. And we'll do it
        in Python, directly in the browser. But getting there is not trivial.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <CubeCompare client:only="react" />
  </section>

  <section id="intro">
    <LogoAnimation
      client:load
      width={2080}
      height={600}
      pixelScale={8}
      duration={2700}
      loop={true}
    />
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        First of all, we need to be precise about what we are trying to measure.
        What do we mean by the smallest number of moves needed to solve a cube?
        Well, that depends on what counts as a move.
      </p>
      <p>
        In the cubing community, each of the six faces is identified by a
        letter. These names are relative to the cube's current orientation. So
        the right face does not mean a fixed colour. It simply means the face
        that is on the right from our current point of view.
      </p>
      <p>
        For example, right now the orange face might be on the right. But if we
        rotate the whole cube, that same orange face might become the front face
        instead.
      </p>
      <p>
        Using this notation, we can say a move R is a clockwise rotation of the
        right face. We can also define the inverse of R as a counter-clockwise
        rotation of the same face.
      </p>
      <p>
        We could say that turning a face by 180 degrees is also a basic move. In
        that case, we call it R2.
      </p>
      <p>
        Now that we have a way to describe moves, we can start thinking about
        the cube as a graph. Each configuration is a node, and there is an edge
        between two nodes if a single allowed move transforms one into the
        other.
      </p>
      <p>
        To see how this works, let us look at a tiny example. Suppose we only
        allow the move R. Starting from the solved cube, applying R once gives
        us a new state. Applying it again gives another one. Applying it twice
        more brings us back to solved.
      </p>
      <p>
        So if we only allow R, the entire reachable universe has just four
        states. This is the full graph generated by that move set.
      </p>
      <p>
        Now look at the distance back to solved. This state is only one move
        away. But this other one is three moves away. The direction of the
        arrows matters.
      </p>
      <p>
        Now let us add the inverse move, R prime. The graph changes immediately.
        Some paths become shorter, because now we can move in both directions.
      </p>
      <p>
        In this new graph, the farthest state is only two moves away from
        solved. And it can be solved in two different ways.
      </p>
      <p>
        And this is exactly why the choice of allowed moves matters. If we also
        allow moves like R2, some shortest paths become even shorter.
      </p>
      <p>
        For example, a state that would be two quarter turns away from solved
        becomes only one move away if a 180 degree turn counts as a single move.
      </p>
      <p>
        So before we talk about the minimum number of moves needed to solve the
        cube, we first need to decide what moves we allow.
      </p>
      <p>
        In the cubing community, if only quarter turns count as single moves,
        this is called the quarter-turn metric. If half turns like R2 also count
        as single moves, this is called the half-turn metric.
      </p>
      <p>
        There is also no need to define R3, because that is exactly the same as
        R prime. So for each face, we really have three meaningful moves:
        clockwise, counter-clockwise, and half turn.
      </p>
      <p>With six faces, that gives us a total of eighteen possible moves.</p>
    </aside>
  </section>

  <section id="part-1">
    <h1 class="text-9xl font-bold">Part 1: Naive</h1>
  </section>

  <section id="cube">
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        The first challenge is representation. Before we can explore the cube,
        we need to describe it in code. We need to encode the position of every
        sticker.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <EncodingShowcase client:only="react" width={1080} height={600} />
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        There are many ways to do this, but a very natural starting point is to
        work with the stickers. We choose a fixed order, and represent the cube
        as a string where each character tells us the colour of one sticker.
      </p>
      <p>
        Every cube state becomes just a different string. That gives us
        something a computer can work with. The next question is: how do we make
        a move?
      </p>
      <p>
        A move does not create new stickers, and it does not change their
        colours. It only moves them around. So every move is just a reordering
        of the string.
      </p>
    </aside>

    <RubikCubeViz
      client:only="react"
      width={1080}
      height={720}
      showStateEditor={true}
      cameraRelativeEncoding={true}
    />
  </section>

  <section id="cube">
    <aside class="notes">
      <pre>
        After around 3 hours and a peak memory consumption of 125GB the execution finishes.
        Total states visited: 88179840
        Move counts by distance:
            {{0: 1, 1: 18, 2: 243, 3: 2874, 4: 28000, 5: 205416, 6: 1168516, 7: 5402628, 8: 20776176, 9: 45391616, 10: 15139616, 11: 64736}}
      </pre>
      <p>Encoding no relativo a la camara.</p>
    </aside>
    <RubikCubeViz
      client:only="react"
      width={1080}
      height={720}
      showStateEditor={true}
      cameraRelativeEncoding={false}
    />
  </section>

  <section id="lehmer-table">
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        If we list every possible permutation of n elements and sort them
        lexicographically, we can assign each one a unique index (from 0 to
        n!-1). This means we can identify any permutation with a single integer,
        which is far more compact than storing the full array of length n.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <LehmerTable
      client:only="react"
      initialN={3}
      showNSelector={true}
      showColumns={[]}
      maxHeight="50vh"
    />
  </section>

  <section id="lehmer-builder">
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        Build a permutation step by step, row by row. At each position you
        choose from the remaining values — the index of that choice in the
        available pool is the corresponding Lehmer digit. Watch the Lehmer code
        and factoriadic index change as you pick each element.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <LehmerBuilder client:only="react" initialN={4} showNSelector={true} />
  </section>

  <section id="lehmer-decoder">
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        Now the reverse direction: given an index, we recover the permutation.
        The slider lets you sweep through all n! indices. For each index we
        perform factoriadic decomposition to get the Lehmer code, then decode it
        step by step: at each position, pick the Lehmer-digit-th element from
        the shrinking pool of available values.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <LehmerDecoder client:only="react" initialN={4} showNSelector={true} />
  </section>

  <section id="corner-invariant">
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        Now we arrive at a deep structural property of the cube. Each of the 8
        corners has an orientation value — 0, 1 or 2 — depending on whether its
        blue/green sticker faces up/down (0), or has twisted clockwise (1) or
        counter-clockwise (2). Apply any sequence of legal moves and watch: the
        individual orientations change freely, but the sum of all 8 values,
        taken modulo 3, is always zero. This is an invariant — a conservation
        law that every legal state must respect.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <CornerInvariant client:only="react" width={420} height={340} />
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p></p>
    </aside>
    <div style={{ height: "80vh" }}>
      <PyodideWorkerRunner client:only="react" initialCode={vectorizedCode} />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="state-graph">
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        Now let's visualise the state space as a graph. Each node is a cube
        state and each edge is a single move connecting two states. You can type
        any state index, adjust the BFS depth with the slider, and explore the
        neighbourhood. Zoom in to see the unfolded cube on each node; zoom out
        and they collapse to dots. Click a node to re-centre the graph on it.
      </p>
    </aside>
